Rights-based constraints (you cannot torture even a guilty person; you cannot punish the innocent even to deter others) exist precisely to prevent 'ends justify means' reasoning from destroying the protections we most value.
Virtue ethics (Aristotle, MacIntyre) argues that performing unethical acts corrupts the agent — you cannot remain a good person while systematically doing bad things, even for good reasons.
The Nuremberg defence — 'I was following orders for a good cause' — was explicitly rejected by the tribunal, establishing that individuals bear moral responsibility for their acts regardless of commanded justification.
